<Docs
  title="GitWarren on your tailnet — live updates, and reviews on your phone"
  description="Let your machines discover each other over Tailscale, get comments the moment they are written, and open a review on your phone with no token to copy."
  eyebrow="Tailnet"
  heading="Your machines, finding each other."
  intro="SSH is the universal way to reach a machine you own, and it needs no extra software. A tailnet buys three things on top of it: machines that appear by themselves, updates that arrive the moment they happen, and a review you can open on your phone."
>
  <h2>Turning it on</h2>

  <p>
    On <strong>Other machines</strong>, under <em>On your tailnet</em>, switch on{" "}
    <strong>Reachable on your tailnet</strong>. GitWarren runs{" "}
    <code>tailscale serve</code> in front of the loopback port it already
    listens on. Nothing new is opened, and nothing is exposed to the internet —{" "}
    <code>tailscale funnel</code> is deliberately never used.
  </p>

  <p>
    You need Tailscale installed and logged in on the machine. GitWarren does
    not bundle it, does not manage it, and works without it.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <span class="note-title">One extra step on Linux</span>
    <p>
      <code>tailscale serve</code> refuses to run without root unless it has
      been told your user may drive it. Once, per machine:
    </p>
    <pre><code>sudo tailscale set --operator=$USER</code></pre>
    <p>
      macOS and Windows both apply it as the ordinary user, so this is a
      Linux-only step. If it has not been done, GitWarren reports what Tailscale
      actually said rather than silently failing to turn the switch on.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Who is allowed in</h2>

  <p>
    Every request that arrives this way has to carry a Tailscale login equal to
    the owner&#39;s — the identity Tailscale itself asserts, taken from{" "}
    <code>tailscale status</code>. Anything else is refused before it reaches
    anything that could answer it.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is no pairing token, no password, and no credential GitWarren issued
    or stores. Authorisation is <em>the same login as the owner</em>, which is
    also why sharing a review with another person is not a thing this does —
    that is GitHub&#39;s job.
  </p>

  <h2>Machines that appear by themselves</h2>

  <p>
    Peers on your tailnet are probed, and the ones running a GitWarren that
    answers are proposed as hosts. You confirm them; nothing is added behind
    your back. Manual entry stays for everything else.
  </p>

  <p>
    A machine you added over ssh and then find again on the tailnet is
    recognised as the same machine rather than listed twice — what settles it is
    the identity the install reports, not the address you happened to type.
  </p>

  <h2>Live updates</h2>

  <p>
    A listening host pushes. Reviews and comments arrive the moment they are
    written, including writes from an agent — when an agent leaves a comment,
    the MCP process pokes the GitWarren that owns the data directory, and the
    change is on your screen about a second later.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is also what greys a machine that goes away: the socket is held open with
    a heartbeat on it, so a host that vanishes is marked offline within a second
    rather than the next time something asks it a question.
  </p>

  <p>
    A fifteen-second poll remains underneath all of it, everywhere. A lost
    update costs you seconds, never correctness.
  </p>

  <h2>Your phone</h2>

  <p>
    Nothing was built for the phone. The same review UI the browser gets on
    loopback is reachable at the host&#39;s tailnet address from any device on
    your tailnet, and Tailscale supplies the identity — so there is no token to
    get onto a phone and nothing to log into.
  </p>

  <pre><code>https://&lt;machine&gt;.&lt;your-tailnet&gt;.ts.net/review/4/conversation</code></pre>

  <p>
    Below a laptop width the files list and the diff become separate screens
    rather than two columns crushed together, and the comment composer sits
    above the keyboard instead of under it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Links an agent prints carry both addresses once a host is listening: a
    loopback one, which always works and depends on nothing, and the tailnet one
    for the device you are actually holding.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <span class="note-title">HTTPS is a tailnet-wide setting</span>
    <p>
      Whether those URLs are <code>https</code> or plain <code>http</code>{" "}
      depends on whether HTTPS certificates are enabled for your tailnet, which
      is a Tailscale setting rather than a GitWarren one. Either way the traffic
      is inside WireGuard, which is encrypting it regardless.
    </p>
  </div>
